  "limitations": [
    "The study was not designed to evaluate the architects' material selection process, but rather the perception of materials.",
    "The roles of tactile, auditory, and olfactory perception during materials selection remain to be established.",
    "The study used small-scale samples, which may not fully represent the perception of full-scale walls.",
    "The study focused only on vision compared to touch, not including all senses.",
    "The findings might imply that professional architects may have similar perceptual biases as naive consumers, as years of training had little effect on assessment."
  ],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This empirical study investigated how architecture students assess building materials using vision versus touch. It found that while students assess several experiential qualities differently by touch than by vision, vision tends to dominate their overall material assessment. Students also struggled to correctly identify common building materials by touch alone and seemed unaware of how they feel. The study highlights that architectural education had little impact on these perceptual biases beyond the first year. Limitations include the use of small-scale samples and the focus only on vision and touch, suggesting a need for future research with larger samples and a broader multisensory approach.",
